<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.5.1: Numerical Patterns</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01812</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.5.1: Numerical Patterns</md:title>
    <md:uuid>7c7c442f-a781-4d92-84f8-ece735015f41</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up Activity</title>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Study the table. What do you notice? What do you wonder?</para>
    
    <table class="os-raise-horizontaltable" id="table-00001">
      <tgroup cols="7"><tbody><row>
          <entry><emphasis>
            \(x\)
         </emphasis> </entry><entry>
            0
          </entry>
          <entry>
            1
          </entry>
          <entry>
            2
          </entry>
          <entry>
            3
          </entry>
          <entry>
            4
          </entry>
          <entry>
            5
          </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry><emphasis>
            \(y\)
         </emphasis> </entry><entry>
            0
          </entry>
          <entry>
            16
          </entry>
          <entry>
            64
          </entry>
          <entry>
            144
          </entry>
          <entry>
            256
          </entry>
          <entry>
            400
          </entry>
        </row>
      </tbody></tgroup></table>
    
    
  
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para/>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Things you may notice:</para>
    
    <list id="list-00001">
      <item>The \(x\)-values increment by 1 as you move across the table.</item>
      <item>The \(y\)-values increase.</item>
      <item>All \(y\)-values are even and divisible by 4, 8, and 16.</item>
      <item>The \(y\)-values are all perfect squares.</item>
      <item>The successive differences between the \(y\)-values are growing.</item>
    </list>
    
    <para>Things you may wonder:</para>
    
    <list id="list-00002">
      <item>Is there a rule for these numbers?</item>
      <item>Do the numbers represent the growth of a geometric pattern?</item>
      <item>Do the numbers represent a quadratic relationship?</item>
      <item>What is the next number in the table?</item>
    </list>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
</section>
</content>
